## Multiple CMD Commands
This is where people get caught off guard — **Docker only honors the last CMD**. So this won't work as expected:

```dockerfile
# ❌ Wrong - only the last one runs
CMD echo "Hello"
CMD echo "World"   # only this runs
```

Instead, chain them together:

```dockerfile
# ✅ Use sh -c with && to run multiple commands
CMD sh -c "echo 'Hello' && echo 'World' && echo 'Done'"
```

Or for cleaner multi-line readability:

```dockerfile
CMD sh -c "\
    echo 'Student: ${STUDENT_NAME}' && \
    echo 'Goal: ${STUDENT_GOAL}' && \
    echo 'Daily hours: ${STUDY_HOURS}' \
"
```

---

## Putting It All Together
```dockerfile
FROM alpine:latest

ENV STUDENT_GOAL="Thinkbook" \
    STUDENT_NAME="John" \
    STUDY_HOURS="8" \
    COURSE="Docker"

CMD sh -c "\
    echo 'Name: ${STUDENT_NAME}' && \
    echo 'Goal: ${STUDENT_GOAL}' && \
    echo 'Course: ${COURSE}' && \
    echo 'Daily Study Hours: ${STUDY_HOURS}' \
"
```

