## CMD vs RUN — Worth Knowing
In learning Docker, this distinction matters:

| | `RUN` | `CMD` |
|---|---|---|
| When it executes | At **build time** | At **container start** |
| How many | As many as you want | Only **one** (last wins) |
| Use case | Installing packages, setup | Starting your app/script |

```dockerfile
FROM alpine:latest

# RUN executes at build time - you can have many
RUN apk add curl
RUN apk add bash

# CMD executes when container starts - only one
CMD echo "Container is live!"
```

If you need to install things, use `RUN`. If you need to execute something when the container starts, use `CMD`.



Same concept as `ENV` — chain them together with `&&` and `\` to keep it one single layer:

```dockerfile
# ❌ Bad - creates a new layer for each RUN
RUN apk add curl
RUN apk add bash
RUN apk add git
RUN apk add vim
```

```dockerfile
# ✅ Good - single layer, single RUN
RUN apk add curl \
    && apk add bash \
    && apk add git \
    && apk add vim
```

Or even cleaner, since `apk add` accepts multiple packages at once:

```dockerfile
# ✅ Best for installing packages
RUN apk add --no-cache \
    curl \
    bash \
    git \
    vim
```

---

## Why `--no-cache`?
On Alpine specifically, `--no-cache` tells apk **not to store the package index** after installing. This keeps your image size smaller — very important on a space-conscious system like yours.

---

## Full Clean Example
```dockerfile
FROM alpine:latest

ENV STUDENT_GOAL="Thinkbook" \
    STUDENT_NAME="John"

# All setup in one layer
RUN apk add --no-cache \
    curl \
    bash \
    git \
    && mkdir /app \
    && echo "setup done"

CMD sh -c "echo 'Hello ${STUDENT_NAME}'"
```

The general rule of thumb is — **one `RUN`, one `ENV`, one `CMD`** — keeps your image lean and your layers minimal.


Really good mindset to have! Here's the breakdown:

---

## `sh` 
This calls the **shell program** itself. On Alpine it's a minimal shell called `ash` but it responds to `sh`. Think of it like saying *"hey shell, I need you to do something"*.

---

## `-c`
This is a flag that means **"read commands from the following string"**. Without `-c`, the shell expects to read commands from a file or interactively from you typing. With `-c` you're saying *"here's the command as a text string, run it"*.

---

## Why Do We Need It At All?
Because Docker's `CMD` on its own is dumb — it can only run **one single executable**. It doesn't understand `&&`, `||`, `\`, or any shell logic by itself.

So when you write:
```dockerfile
# Docker tries to run this as a raw executable - fails on && 
CMD echo "Hello" && echo "World"
```

Docker doesn't know what `&&` means. But when you write:
```dockerfile
# You're handing the whole string to the shell to interpret
CMD sh -c "echo 'Hello' && echo 'World'"
```
You're essentially launching a shell and letting **the shell** handle all the logic — it understands `&&`, variables, `||`, everything.

---

## Simple Analogy
Think of it like this:

| | Analogy |
|---|---|
| `sh` | Hiring a translator |
| `-c` | Giving them a written note to translate |
| `"echo hello && echo world"` | The note itself |

Without the translator (`sh -c`), Docker reads `&&` and goes *"I don't know what this means"*. With it, the shell handles everything fluently.

---

## You Can Test This Right Now
In the terminal, try:
```bash
sh -c "echo 'Hello' && echo 'World'"
```
You're doing exactly what Docker does internally when it hits that `CMD` line — spawning a shell and passing it a string to execute.
